1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,500
The letter says $450,000. More money than most people have ever seen in

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:12,060
one place. Yours in one check. If you give up your pension for it.

3
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:14,660
And at the bottom of the letter, there's a deadline.

4
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:19,740
So before that date comes, let's put the two side by side.

5
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:24,240
The check you'd get every month for the rest of your life and the pile you'd

6
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:29,160
get once. Then four tests. Which one pays you more each month?

7
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:32,180
What the number in the letter is really built on?

8
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:35,560
What inflation does to each one over 20 years?

9
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:38,640
And who carries the risk when something goes wrong?

10
00:00:39,340 --> 00:00:43,720
Nothing here is advice. It's a worked example with every assumption set

11
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:46,600
out loud. So you can put your own letter next to it.

12
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:50,020
Here's the person we're using. 65.

13
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:55,280
Single. Retiring this year. The pension pays $40,000 a year for life.

14
00:00:55,580 --> 00:01:01,120
Which is $3,333 a month. That's called a single life pension.

15
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:04,800
It pays as long as you're alive and it stops when you die.

16
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:08,720
Like most private pensions, it has no cost of living increase.

17
00:01:09,280 --> 00:01:11,660
The check on day one is the check at 90.

18
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:14,600
And the plan is offering a lump sum instead.

19
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:20,380
For this example, $450,000. That figure isn't pulled from the air.

20
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:24,760
Using the interest rates the IRS published for July 2026,

21
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:30,100
a $40,000 pension at 65 works out to a lump sum in that neighborhood.

22
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:32,240
Your letter will have its own number.

23
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:35,540
And in the second test, you'll see exactly why it moves.

24
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:37,960
Why would a company offer this at all?

25
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:42,140
Because a pension is a promise it has to keep funding for decades.

26
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:44,860
And a lump sum ends the promise today.

27
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:49,060
That's not sinister. It's the company moving a risk off its books.

28
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:52,300
The question is whether you want that risk on yours.

29
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:57,080
One more thing before the math. Read the line about the deadline twice.

30
00:01:57,740 --> 00:02:01,700
Find out what happens if you do nothing and whether the choice can be undone.

31
00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:05,700
Once the lump sum is paid, the pension is gone for good.

32
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:09,300
There's no buying it back. Let's start with the paycheck.

33
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:14,520
Test one. If you take the $450,000,

34
00:02:15,260 --> 00:02:18,560
how much can it pay you each month? Last episode,

35
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:22,140
we used the 4% rule. Pull 4% in year one,

36
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:26,740
raise it with inflation, and history says the money usually lasts 30 years.

37
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:31,600
4% of $450,000 is $18,000 a year.

38
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:37,180
$1,500 a month. The pension pays $3,333.

39
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:40,540
Same money. Less than half the paycheck.

40
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:45,720
And at the cautious 3 .5%, the lump sum pays about $1,300 a month.

41
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:49,860
Now, that's not quite a fair fight. And it's worth saying why.

42
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:54,460
The 4% rule is built to last 30 years and rise with inflation.

43
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:58,120
The pension never rises, and it stops when you do.

44
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:01,760
So let's make the lump sum do exactly what the pension does.

45
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:06,100
Pay $40,000 a year, flat, and see how long it lasts.

46
00:03:06,720 --> 00:03:09,980
To match the pension, you'd be pulling almost 9% a year.

47
00:03:10,580 --> 00:03:14,360
If the money earns nothing, it's gone in 11 years and 3 months,

48
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:20,000
age 76. If it earns 4% a year, it runs out a little before age 80.

49
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:26,440
At 6%, about 83. At 7% every single year with no bad stretches,

50
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:30,820
age 86. So the real question is how long you're going to need it.

51
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:34,700
The Social Security Administration's own life table answers that,

52
00:03:35,020 --> 00:03:38,680
averaged across men and women. Of everyone who reaches 65,

53
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:41,640
about 7 in 10 are still alive at 80.

54
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:45,800
About half reach 85. And about 3 in 10 reach 90.

55
00:03:46,330 --> 00:03:51,560
The averages, from the same agency, are about 84 for a man reaching 65 in

56
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:57,220
2026, and about 87 for a woman. And half of everyone outlives the average.

57
00:03:57,920 --> 00:03:59,780
Think about what that means for the lump sum.

58
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:03,260
At a steady 6%, it runs out around 83.

59
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:07,560
More than half the people holding that check are still alive after it's

60
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:10,800
empty. The pension doesn't care which half you turn out to be.

61
00:04:11,140 --> 00:04:13,680
It keeps paying. How to check yours.

62
00:04:14,320 --> 00:04:16,540
Find the monthly pension figure in your letter,

63
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:19,600
multiply by 12, and divide by the lump sum.

64
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:22,920
That's the withdrawal rate the lump sum has to support.

65
00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:26,120
If that number is above about 6 or 7%,

66
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:29,020
the money has to earn a lot, every single year,

67
00:04:29,120 --> 00:04:32,940
just to keep up. Then, be honest about the other half of the math,

68
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:36,600
which is you, your health. How long your parents lived.

69
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:40,140
A pension is really insurance against living a long time.

70
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:43,180
It's worth the most to the people who end up needing it.

71
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:48,980
Test 2. Where does the number in the letter actually come from?

72
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:52,280
It isn't the company's opinion of what your pension is worth.

73
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:55,520
There's a formula, and the IRS sets the ingredients.

74
00:04:56,200 --> 00:05:00,080
Two of them. A life table for how long people your age tend to live.

75
00:05:00,580 --> 00:05:03,780
And a set of interest rates, based on corporate bond yields,

76
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:08,020
that the IRS publishes every month. The rates are the part that moves.

77
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:11,900
There are three of them. One for the payments in your first five years,

78
00:05:11,900 --> 00:05:15,540
one for the next 15, and one for everything after that.

79
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:20,600
For July 2026, they ran from about 4 .5% on the near years,

80
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:24,360
to about 6 .5% on the far ones. The math works backwards.

81
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:28,020
It asks, how much money today, earning those rates,

82
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:30,620
would cover every check you're expected to collect?

83
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:33,420
That's your lump sum. Here's the catch.

84
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:36,440
When rates go up, the lump sum goes down.

85
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:40,840
Same pension, same person. If those rates were one point lower,

86
00:05:41,140 --> 00:05:43,920
this offer would be about $480,000.

87
00:05:44,020 --> 00:05:48,760
One point higher, about $410,000. So two people with identical pensions

88
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:53,340
can get offers $70,000 apart, just by retiring in different years.

89
00:05:54,060 --> 00:05:56,060
Neither offer is a trick. It's arithmetic.

90
00:05:56,740 --> 00:05:59,860
But it does mean the rate in your letter matters as much as the pension

91
00:05:59,860 --> 00:06:02,620
it's replacing. You can't negotiate the formula.

92
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:06,280
You can check that it was applied. Test three.

93
00:06:06,940 --> 00:06:10,560
Inflation. And this is where the lump sum wins one back.

94
00:06:11,260 --> 00:06:14,160
The pension is flat. At 3% inflation,

95
00:06:14,460 --> 00:06:16,180
which is roughly the long run average,

96
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:22,160
$40,000 at 65 buys what about $24,000 buys today by the time you're 82.

97
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:27,760
At 85, about $22,000. At 90, about $19,000.

98
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:31,460
The check looks the same. It just buys less every year.

99
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:36,400
The lump sum, drawn down at 4%, is designed to rise with prices.

100
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:42,180
That $1,500 a month at 65 becomes about $2,480 a month by 82.

101
00:06:42,820 --> 00:06:45,360
Smaller to start. But it keeps its value.

102
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:48,480
And whatever's left when you die goes to whoever you name.

103
00:06:49,060 --> 00:06:51,280
A single life pension leaves nothing behind.

104
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:55,560
If heirs matter to you, or you want a cushion you control for a new roof

105
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:58,480
or a hospital bill, that counts. It's real.

106
00:06:59,500 --> 00:07:03,720
How to check yours. Your election packet is required to show what each option

107
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:05,700
is worth compared to the standard pension.

108
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:10,540
Look for the words relative value and ask the plan which month's interest

109
00:07:10,540 --> 00:07:13,120
rates they used and when that rate resets.

110
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:17,300
Test four. When something goes wrong,

111
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:22,580
who pays? Take the pension and the company carries the market risk and the

112
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:25,600
risk that you live to a hundred. If the company fails,

113
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:28,000
the Pension Benefit Guaranty Corporation,

114
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:31,740
a federal agency, steps in. For 2026,

115
00:07:32,020 --> 00:07:37,540
it guarantees up to about $7,790 a month for someone starting at 65.

116
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:42,140
The limit is lower if payments start younger and higher if they start later.

117
00:07:42,660 --> 00:07:44,720
This pension is well under that line.

118
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:48,020
Take the lump sum and all of that risk is yours.

119
00:07:48,820 --> 00:07:53,500
Here's what that looks like. Pull $40,000 a year and earn 6% every year

120
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:57,380
and the money lasts 17 years. Now give it one bad year,

121
00:07:57,540 --> 00:08:01,680
down 20% right at the start. Same 6% after that.

122
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:07,180
It lasts 11 years. Put that same bad year 10 years in and it lasts 15.

123
00:08:07,820 --> 00:08:10,600
The order of the returns matters as much as the average.

124
00:08:11,280 --> 00:08:13,200
Two more traps sit inside the paperwork.

125
00:08:13,960 --> 00:08:18,880
First, taxes. Have the plan move the money straight to an IRA called a direct

126
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:23,060
rollover and nothing is taxed yet. But if the check is made out to you,

127
00:08:23,320 --> 00:08:28,300
the plan must withhold 20%. That's $90,000 sent to the IRS.

128
00:08:28,860 --> 00:08:33,140
You have 60 days to put the full $450,000 into an IRA,

129
00:08:33,620 --> 00:08:37,500
which means finding that $90,000 from your own pocket or it gets taxed

130
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:41,940
as income. After that, the tax is a wash in one way and different in

131
00:08:41,940 --> 00:08:45,720
another. Pension checks are taxed as ordinary income as they arrive.

132
00:08:46,340 --> 00:08:49,080
Money you pull from the IRA is taxed the same way.

133
00:08:49,560 --> 00:08:52,220
The difference is control. With the IRA,

134
00:08:52,540 --> 00:08:54,480
you decide how much to take each year,

135
00:08:54,720 --> 00:08:56,740
at least until required withdrawals start.

136
00:08:56,740 --> 00:09:00,720
For someone born in 1960 or later, that's age 75.

137
00:09:01,460 --> 00:09:03,700
And if you're retiring before 59 and a half,

138
00:09:04,040 --> 00:09:07,560
there's a wrinkle. Money taken straight from the plan after you leave the

139
00:09:07,560 --> 00:09:12,680
job in or after the year you turn 55 can avoid the 10% early withdrawal

140
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:17,360
penalty. Roll it into an IRA first and that exception generally no longer

141
00:09:17,360 --> 00:09:21,920
applies. Second, if you're married, the default isn't a single life pension

142
00:09:21,920 --> 00:09:24,600
at all. It's a joint and survivor pension,

143
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:27,700
so your spouse keeps getting at least half after you die.

144
00:09:28,160 --> 00:09:31,500
That protection costs something. With a spouse the same age,

145
00:09:31,780 --> 00:09:36,180
a fair conversion would take this $40,000 down to about $36,000 a year

146
00:09:36,180 --> 00:09:40,240
for a half survivor benefit. Your plan's letter lists its own figures.

147
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:44,820
And to take the lump sum instead, your spouse has to sign away that right

148
00:09:44,820 --> 00:09:48,300
in writing, witnessed by a notary or a plan representative.

149
00:09:49,060 --> 00:09:51,620
It's a decision for two people, not one.

150
00:09:52,940 --> 00:09:58,600
So, four tests. The paycheck goes to the pension by more than double.

151
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:02,520
The number in the letter depends on interest rates you don't control.

152
00:10:03,160 --> 00:10:07,740
Inflation goes to the lump sum and the risk moves from the company to you

153
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:11,240
the moment you sign. Neither answer is right for everyone.

154
00:10:11,720 --> 00:10:15,460
The pension tends to fit people in good health from long -lived families

155
00:10:15,460 --> 00:10:17,500
who'd lose sleep over a market drop.

156
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:21,260
The lump sum tends to fit people with other income for the basics.

157
00:10:21,260 --> 00:10:24,560
A shorter outlook or someone they want to leave money to.

158
00:10:25,180 --> 00:10:28,640
Before you sign either way, answer three questions on paper.

159
00:10:29,540 --> 00:10:34,320
One, add up your guaranteed income. Social security plus this pension if

160
00:10:34,320 --> 00:10:37,240
you keep it. Does that cover the bills that can't be skipped?

161
00:10:37,860 --> 00:10:40,400
Housing, food, insurance, utilities?

162
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:43,080
If the pension is what makes that line work,

163
00:10:43,560 --> 00:10:46,560
losing it is a bigger deal than the lump sum makes it look.

164
00:10:47,060 --> 00:10:51,380
Two, if you're married, write down what your spouse would receive each month

165
00:10:51,380 --> 00:10:53,620
under every option after you're gone.

166
00:10:54,080 --> 00:10:59,120
Not just what you receive. Three, ask the plan whether it's all or nothing.

167
00:10:59,640 --> 00:11:04,080
Some plans allow other choices like a partial lump sum or a different survivor

168
00:11:04,080 --> 00:11:06,880
percentage. The letter doesn't always lead with them.

169
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:13,400
One thing to do this week. Ask an insurance company what $450,000 or whatever

170
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:16,380
your letter says would buy as an income for life.

171
00:11:16,380 --> 00:11:18,940
If the answer is less than your pension pays,

172
00:11:19,460 --> 00:11:21,780
you've learned what the plan's offer is really worth.

173
00:11:22,520 --> 00:11:26,960
A CPA or a fee -only fiduciary can run your own letter with your own health

174
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:29,180
and your own taxes in about an hour.

175
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:31,720
If you want the next number worked the same way,

176
00:11:32,060 --> 00:11:33,000
subscribe.

177
00:11:34,170 --> 00:11:36,470
Will your money last as long as you do?

178
00:11:37,220 --> 00:11:39,570
Run your own number. See you Tuesday.
